WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:05.500
Hey Wisecrack.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.820
This is Jared, clown prince of Wisecrack

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:12.420
and today we’re talking about the guy people have in mind when they say that Batman has all the best villains,

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.520
everyone’s favorite nihilist — the Joker.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.060
Making his first appearance in 1940’s Batman #1,

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.840
the world’s busiest evil clown has been featured in hundreds of comics and cartoons,

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.480
dozens of video games, and four live-action films.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:34.900
Inspired by Victor Hugo’s hero Gwynplaine
in the 1928 film adaptation of The Man Who Laughs,

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:47.440
the Joker’s motives have ranged from the sadistic to the mercenary to the downright inexplicable.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.220
But despite the fact that hundreds of creative
minds have worked on the Joker,

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.260
his outlook on the world has been surprisingly consistent

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:59.980
and it represents an extreme version of a reaction we’ve all felt towards the world, at one point or another.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:04.760
So put a smile on that face and enjoy this
Wisecrack Edition on the Philosophy of the Joker.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.820
There are a lot of explanations for just how
the Joker got to be the Joker.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:18.380
The first account of the Joker’s origin was revealed in Detective Comics #168 back in 1951,

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:23.120
and over the past six decades most versions have followed more or less the same plotline:

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.060
a two-bit villain known as the Red
Hood loses a fight to Batman,

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.720
falls into a vat of skin-bleaching and hair-brightening
chemicals,

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.060
and emerges as something entirely new.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.400
Over time, DC built on this origin story.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:38.840
Alan Moore’s 1986 magnum opus The Killing
Joke made the Joker’s origin a lot more tragic,

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:43.380
making him a reluctant villain who
lost his humanity after “One Bad Day” —

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:51.960
where he failed as a stand-up comic, was informed of the death of his pregnant wife, got framed by the mob,

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.700
and only then fell into a vat
of disfiguring chemicals.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:02.540
The entire premise of The Killing Joke was that the Joker hoped to turn Commissioner Gordon into another version of himself

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:09.060
by giving Gordon a similarly bad day — to prove that even the best of us are never more than one bad day away from becoming,

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.440
well, whatever the Joker is.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:11.440
More on that later.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.780
While The Killing Joke is technically not
a canonical story,

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:20.860
and the Joker gives a strong indication that he has no idea whether this version of his history is accurate anyway,

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:30.660
the comics, animated series, and games have mostly stuck by Alan Moore’s depressing version of the Joker’s origins ever since.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.980
Batman and the Joker have something in common:

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:39.880
Both live their lives as responses to traumatic, world-view-shattering events,

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:44.400
events that represent an existential crisis that most people have to deal with sooner or later:

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.320
How do you respond when life destroys your sense of meaning?

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:52.240
Batman reacts to his parents’ death by dressing up in a bat-eared leotard and battering criminals,

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.980
which seems reasonable enough by comic book standards.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.360
But the Joker’s One Bad Day led him, with equal passion, to embrace meaninglessness and violence —

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:05.360
after all, if moral nihilism is correct, then nothing is right or wrong.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:08.380
According to the French existentialist philosopher Jean- Paul Sartre,

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:14.840
recognizing the meaninglessness of existence forces us to realize that we are responsible for creating our own meaning.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:18.780
Choosing how to deal with this loss of meaning
animates the entire Batman universe.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:22.840
Batman decides to uphold some form of conventional
morality as a vigilante;

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.180
the Joker chooses to abandon it
entirely;

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.400
and the villain Two-Face splits the difference,

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:32.180
literally flipping a coin to decide whether he’ll uphold conventional morality or rebel against it.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:37.840
Although the Batman persona is usually presented
as a response to personal trauma, not nihilism,

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.800
more recent versions of the character have
begun to sound more and more existential.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:58.100
The Joker is aware of this distinction between
himself and The Dark Knight,

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:01.840
and in recent iterations of the character, he even feels
a kinship with him.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:15.200
This places Batman and the Joker in an existential struggle:

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.700
Batman is trying to convince the Joker to accept conventional morality,

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:22.100
while the Joker is trying to seduce Batman into abandoning it.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:25.540
1989’s Batman connected the characters even
more directly,

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:29.720
making two-bit mobster Jack Napier the original killer of Batman’s parents.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:34.400
And it was Batman who later sent Napier tumbling
into the chemical vat, turning him into the Joker.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:35.920
They gave birth to each other.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:46.960
In Nolan’s The Dark Knight (2005), Batman and the Joker are already fully-formed by the time they meet.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:52.520
Heath Ledger’s Joker was different from any version that preceded him — different from any other villain in cinema, really.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:58.500
As an elemental force of chaos with supernatural
charisma, planning, and dumb luck,

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:01.180
he didn’t have or need a consistent origin story.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:06.220
He was more of a demon summoned by Batman’s
enemies to fight him.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.240
A demon they soon learned that they couldn't
control.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:26.880
In other words, in the world of The Dark Knight, the Joker’s presence in Gotham City is entirely Batman’s fault.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:34.240
By introducing oppressive justice and order,

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.340
he has thrown the moral universe of Gotham out of balance

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:40.880
and created opportunity for a new kind of chaos and evil to emerge.

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:50.980
This idea of society without balance is central to the philosophy of French sociologist Jean Baudrillard.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:56.620
In The Transparency of Evil, he argues that the kind of moral progress Batman seeks would be impossible

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:00.620
because even as he seeks to uphold
justice by making criminals his bitch,

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:10.080
he creates a more extreme versions of order for
criminals to challenge and subvert —

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.140
thus giving birth to a radically antithetical version
of Batman’s brand of justice — The Joker.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:18.200
In trying to completely sanitize Gotham of evil,

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:23.480
Batman essentially created a supergerm, a kind of autoimmunity disease in the form of The Joker.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:35.800
In Baudrillard’s words:

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:38.840
According to Baudriallard, evil isn’t
the opposite of good.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:42.280
If something unpleasant happens to you, that
doesn’t mean it's evil.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:45.860
Evil is characterized by a radical indifference
to good.

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:47.620
This is essentially The Joker.

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:51.280
Instead of merely making bad things happen
to people like other supervillains,

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:56.920
the Joker has a more sinister goal: to completely destroy
the concept of what “good” even means.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:05.740
Nobody ever labels themselves as “evil,”
everyone considers themself to be good.

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:10.920
So the Joker tries to seduce Batman, Gordon,
and others into abandoning their moral foundations

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:16.320
and do something they’d never see themselves doing, thereby corrupting their understanding of “goodness.”

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:23.040
His mischievous passion for breaking down people’s confidence in the division between good and evil

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.680
is EXACTLY what makes the Joker
so intriguing.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:28.160
Most other villains combat good with the opposite.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:30.180
The Joker destroys good.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:33.560
In the end, Batman wins the fight, but the
Joker wins the argument:

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:38.860
The once idealistic Harvey Dent is now completely indifferent to the distinction between good and evil.

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:51.980
Whether he’s trying to leave his deadly imprint on the world around him or just break down one character’s moral compass,

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:08.820
the Joker’s objective seems to be to show us that our pretenses to love and morality are phony,

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:11.200
and that we’re a lot more self-serving than we let on.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:15.940
But I suppose the question still remains:
why does he bother?

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:18.520
It’s a cop-out to say the Joker is mentally ill.

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:20.920
The common classification of the Joker as insane

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:25.700
seems to just be a way of demonstrating how little we understand about how his mind works,

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:29.700
and why it takes a similarly dark and eccentric character to figure him out.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:34.780
What makes the Joker violent, in other words,
isn’t a psychiatric problem; it’s an existential one.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:39.400
He is pathologically sane, and he's seen
how fragile conventional morality is.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:49.400
He believes that no one is more than one sufficiently
bad day away from total depravity.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:52.660
But the Joker doesn’t just abandon traditional
moral codes;

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:55.680
he subverts them with a sinister kind of humor.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:01.380
The Joker is a joker, just like it says on
the tin.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:07.500
He resembles a clown, he has a huge permanent grin, and he’s been known to use elaborate, slapstick-based murder weapons.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:11.340
It’s his gimmick.

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:14.320
And for most of the character’s history, it’s
been just a gimmick.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:17.120
But starting with the publication of The Killing
Joke in 1988,

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:21.960
and continuing through the release of The Dark Knight in 2008, there’s been a darker undertone to it.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:24.360
He enjoys doing wretched, horrifying stuff.

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:25.560
It’s funny to him.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:29.420
The Joker jokes because, from his point of
view, everything is trivial.

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:37.940
And while it’s easy to write him off as
a sadist,

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:44.100
his regular attempts to tempt people into doing things that upset their own sense of right and wrong and reveal their hypocrisy

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:46.860
may speak to the very heart of what humor is.

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:52.300
In Book I of his masterpiece The World as
Will and Representation,

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:56.780
German Philosopher and Prodigy “Firestarter” hair model Arthur Schopenhauer wrote that:

00:09:56.780 --> 00:10:12.260
 

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:18.480
The idea of finding humor in nihilism isn’t really new, and
in smaller doses it can be a great survival mechanism.

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:21.620
In Laughing at Nothing: Humor as a Response to Nihilism,

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:27.480
the contemporary American philosopher John Marmysz argues that laughing at the void can be a really healthy thing to do:

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:51.420
Because the Joker is an active and even joyfully fanatical nihilist,

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:56.000
who sees it as his duty to confront imposed moral order with the reality of chaos,

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:02.660
his entire life revolves around identifying incongruities between what we say we are (moral, righteous, GOOD citizens)

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:07.020
and what we actually are (selfish, savage creatures) —

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:11.880
and he’s having a great time doing it.

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:17.680
According to the Joker, conventional morality and the social structures built around those ideas are flimsy and weak.

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:21.480
He believes that nobody is ever more than one bad
day away from abandoning it.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:27.860
His entire career as a villain centers on
giving people that one bad day he thinks they need to end up just like him.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:39.360
The Joker’s signature weapon, Joker venom,
is a deadly gas that kills its victims

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:45.040
by forcing them to laugh themselves to death while their face contorts into a rictus grin just like his own.

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:51.380
And Joker venom is as old as the character;
he used it in his debut appearance in 1940’s Batman #1,

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:54.060
and most versions of the Joker
use some version of it.

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:05.300
Proving everyone’s capable of becoming what he is, both morally and physically —

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:07.700
it's the Joker’s greatest pleasure and obsession.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:14.560
And it has made the character terrifying and
relevant for most of the past century.

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:16.140
It’s his killing joke.

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:21.920
It’s hard to resist the temptation to glamorize
the Joker a little bit,

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:26.340
partly because he’s been around so long that we’ve developed a certain amount of affection for the guy.

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:32.100
And when you look at the scale of human suffering,
injustice, hypocrisy, and greed,

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:36.440
it’s easy to relate to the Joker’s commitment to proving
we’re not as noble as we pretend to be.

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:39.220
But he isn’t heroic either, and he isn’t supposed to be.

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:44.300
There have been some stories where the Joker did
seemingly heroic things that were consistent with his character.

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:51.280
In the 1996 Marvel/DC crossover Batman &amp; Captain
America, he turned against Red Skull and refused to join the Third Reich.

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:54.400
And in the 2006 conclusion of DC’s Infinite Crisis,

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:59.300
he brutally executed the near-omnipotent Lex Luthor for being too much of a control freak.

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:03.260
But in both cases writers cleverly avoided
having him turn over a new leaf,

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:08.140
giving him plausible selfish motives that justified his
uncharacteristic helpfulness.

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:15.340
But the Joker wouldn’t make a very effective villain if we didn’t all have a little bit of him in us.

00:13:15.340 --> 00:13:20.640
We’re suspicious of other people’s moral codes, we’re liable to fixate on their hypocrisy at the expense of our own,

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:24.080
and we all have moments where we just kind of want to watch the world burn.

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:25.500
He’s still smiling at us.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:27.140
And we’re still smiling back.

